    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Down payment assistance in Texas comes from two statewide sources, TSAHC and TDHCA, which offer up to 5% of your loan amount, plus local programs that can be far larger. In the Houston area, the City of Houston offers up to $75,000 to eligible first-time buyers inside city limits, and Harris County lists up to $40,000 for homes in unincorporated areas, though the county program is limited right now.</p>
<p>This guide focuses on how the money works, what each program asks of you, and the trade-offs. If you are still learning the overall buying process, start with our <a href="/blog/first-time-home-buyer-texas/">first time home buyer Texas guide</a>.</p>

<h2>How down payment assistance works</h2>
<p>Most assistance is not a check you receive. It is money applied at closing toward your down payment, closing costs or prepaids, usually recorded as a second lien behind your main mortgage. You come out with two loans on the property, but the second one typically has no monthly payment.</p>
<p>The structure determines what you owe later:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Monthly payment?</th><th>When you owe it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Grant</td><td>No</td><td>Never</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forgivable second lien</td><td>No</td><td>Only if you sell, refinance, rent it out or move out before the forgiveness period ends</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deferred repayable second lien</td><td>No</td><td>In full when you sell, refinance or pay off the first mortgage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pro rata forgivable lien</td><td>No</td><td>A shrinking share if you leave early; nothing once the full period ends</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The key questions for any program: how long you must live in the home, what triggers repayment, and whether you need permission to refinance later.</p>

<h2>Statewide programs: TSAHC</h2>
<p>The Texas State Affordable Housing Corporation runs two home loan programs, both offering down payment assistance of up to 5% of the loan amount:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Homes for Texas Heroes</strong> for full-time K-12 educators, peace officers, firefighters, EMS personnel, corrections officers and county jailers, public security officers, veterans, and nursing and allied health faculty.</li>
<li><strong>Home Sweet Texas</strong> for low- and moderate-income buyers in any profession.</li>
</ul>
<p>As of 2026, TSAHC offers the assistance as either a grant that never has to be repaid or a forgivable second lien that is forgiven after three years if you do not sell, refinance or move out. There is also a no-assistance option with a lower interest rate. TSAHC lists a minimum credit score of 620 for government loans and 640 for conventional loans, requires a homebuyer education course, and does not always require you to be a first-time buyer.</p>

<h2>Statewide programs: TDHCA</h2>
<p>The Texas Department of Housing and Community Affairs runs the Texas Homebuyer Program through approved loan officers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>My First Texas Home</strong> is for first-time buyers and qualified veterans. It offers up to 5% in down payment and closing cost help.</li>
<li><strong>My Choice Texas Home</strong> is open to first-time buyers, repeat buyers and veterans, with higher income limits than My First Texas Home and the same up-to-5% assistance.</li>
</ul>
<p>TDHCA's assistance is a 0% interest second lien. Its lender guide describes two versions: a 30-year deferred lien that you repay when you sell, refinance or pay off the first mortgage, and a 3-year deferred forgivable lien. Both programs list a 620 minimum credit score, income and purchase price limits by county, and a required homebuyer education course. TDHCA also partners with some local housing finance corporations and cities to add gift funds in certain areas, so ask your loan officer whether any apply where you are buying.</p>

<h2>City of Houston Homebuyer Assistance Program</h2>
<p>The City of Houston Housing and Community Development Department runs the Homebuyer Assistance Program (HAP) for first-time buyers purchasing a new or existing home inside city limits. Under the city's HAP guidelines (version 8.0, dated June 2026):</p>
<ul>
<li>Assistance is up to <strong>$75,000</strong>, structured as a 0% interest forgivable loan secured by a second lien, with no monthly payments. The city sizes each award to the buyer's financial need using a subsidy layering analysis of your income, loan estimate and underwriting, so not everyone receives the maximum.</li>
<li>Funds can go toward the down payment, reasonable closing costs, principal reduction, interest rate reduction and approved prepaids such as insurance and property taxes.</li>
<li>Income limits are 80% of area median income for CDBG-funded assistance and 120% for assistance funded through TIRZ dollars.</li>
<li>You must complete an 8-hour homebuyer education course with a HUD-approved counseling agency and contribute at least $350 of your own money.</li>
<li>The loan is forgiven pro rata: over 5 years for assistance up to $50,000, and over 8 years for assistance above $50,000 up to $75,000, as long as the home stays your principal residence.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because the HAP is funded with federal and local dollars, funding availability and rules can change. Confirm the current terms with the city and a participating lender before you count on it.</p>

<h2>Harris County Down Payment Assistance Program</h2>
<p>Harris County Housing and Community Development runs a separate program for homes in unincorporated Harris County. Its 2026 fact sheet lists:</p>
<ul>
<li>A forgivable second lien of up to <strong>$40,000</strong>, repayable only on default, sale, rental, transfer or refinance during the affordability period.</li>
<li>A primary-residence requirement of 5 years for assistance under $25,000 and 10 years for $25,000 to $40,000.</li>
<li>First-time buyers only (no ownership in the past three years), household income at or below 80% of area median income, and a minimum 580 credit score.</li>
<li>Maximum sales prices of $285,000 for existing homes and $296,000 for new construction, with homes outside flood hazard areas.</li>
<li>An 8-hour homeownership course, a front-end ratio no higher than 39% and a total debt-to-income ratio no higher than 42%.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Current status:</strong> the county's program page says it is accepting only applicants affected by Hurricane Beryl and the derecho, because it has reached capacity for everyone else. Check the page or call before planning around it.</p>

<h2>Income limits, price limits and other fine print</h2>
<p>Every program here caps household income, and most cap the purchase price. The limits vary by county and household size and are updated regularly, so we are not listing figures that may be stale by the time you read this. Ask a participating lender to check your household against the current limits for Harris, Fort Bend, Montgomery or whichever county you are buying in.</p>
<p>Other details that trip buyers up:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Household income, not just borrower income.</strong> Some programs count income from every adult who will live in the home.</li>
<li><strong>Education certificates expire.</strong> Take the course close enough to closing that your certificate is still valid.</li>
<li><strong>Property rules.</strong> Local programs may exclude flood hazard areas, require inspections or limit property types.</li>
<li><strong>Timing.</strong> Local programs can take weeks to approve, so build that into your contract dates.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pros and cons of down payment assistance</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Buy sooner with less cash saved</td><td>Assisted first mortgages can carry a slightly higher rate than unassisted loans</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can cover closing costs as well as the down payment</td><td>A second lien can complicate refinancing or selling early</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forgivable liens disappear if you stay long enough</td><td>Income, price and occupancy rules limit your choices</td></tr>
<tr><td>Local programs can also buy down your rate or principal</td><td>Extra paperwork and approval time; funds can run out</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A smart test: ask your lender to quote the loan with and without assistance. Compare the cash you save upfront against the higher monthly payment and any repayment obligation over the years you expect to own the home. Run both payments through our <a href="/tools/home-affordability-calculator/">home affordability calculator</a>.</p>
[[tool:closing-cost-calculator]]

<h2>Steps to get down payment assistance in Texas</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Check your basics.</strong> Pull your credit, estimate your household income, and decide roughly where you want to buy. Location determines which local programs you can use.</li>
<li><strong>Find a participating lender.</strong> You apply for TSAHC and TDHCA help through approved lenders, and local programs work through their own lender lists. Ask each lender which programs they actually close.</li>
<li><strong>Pick your loan type.</strong> Assistance sits on top of an FHA, VA, USDA or conventional loan. Our guide to <a href="/blog/fha-loan-requirements-texas/">FHA loan requirements in Texas</a> covers the most common pairing.</li>
<li><strong>Take homebuyer education.</strong> Complete the course the program requires and save the certificate.</li>
<li><strong>Get pre-approved with the assistance built in.</strong> Make sure your pre-approval letter reflects the program, price limit and cash to close.</li>
<li><strong>Shop within the limits.</strong> Tell your agent your price cap and any property restrictions, such as flood zones or city limits.</li>
<li><strong>Read the lien documents.</strong> Before closing, review the note and deed of trust for the forgiveness period and repayment triggers, and keep copies.</li>
</ol>
[[product:accordion-file-organizer]]
<p>Program terms change often, so verify everything with a participating lender, and talk to a licensed financial professional if you are weighing assistance against a higher rate.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Down payment assistance in Texas is real money, but it comes with strings. TSAHC and TDHCA cover up to 5% statewide, while the City of Houston's up-to-$75,000 HAP is the biggest local option for first-time buyers inside the city, and Harris County's program is currently limited to storm-affected applicants. Compare the total cost with and without assistance, read the lien terms, and confirm current funding before you write an offer.</p>
